<% content_for :title, "How it works" %>

<div class="page-head">
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p>This bulletin board is built entirely on <strong>ECS Rails</strong> — every
     screen you've seen maps to a component-based domain model.</p>
</div>

<div class="stack">
  <div class="card">
    <h3 class="section-title">Entities are composed from components</h3>
    <p class="prose">A <code>Post</code> isn't a wide table. It's an identity row
       composed from <strong>catalogue</strong> components — standard parts that
       ship with the gem, each under a <em>slot</em> that names its role:</p>
    <pre style="background:var(--surface-2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-s);padding:1rem;overflow:auto"><code>class Post &lt; ApplicationEntity
  component Text,    prefix: :title                               # post.title_text
  component Text,    prefix: :body                                # post.body_text
  component State,   prefix: :publish, states: %w[draft published] # post.publish_state
  component Counter, prefix: :likes                               # post.likes_counter
  relates_to :author, User                                        # post.author
end</code></pre>
    <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">This whole board was written
       <strong>after</strong> <code>rails g ecs_rails:install</code> and one
       <code>db:migrate</code>, and never needed another migration: the
       <code>db/migrate</code> directory holds exactly one file. A post's title, its
       body and a group's rules all live in one <code>texts</code> table; what tells
       them apart is the slot.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid--2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3 class="section-title">Markers → badges</h3>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">The <span class="badge badge--mod">Moderator</span>
         and <span class="badge badge--admin">★ Admin</span> badges on
         <%= link_to "People", users_path %> are marker components. A user
         <em>is</em> a moderator exactly when the row exists — declared with
         <code>marker :moderator</code>, set with <code>user.add(:moderator)</code>,
         asked with <code>user.moderator?</code>. One <code>markers</code> table for
         every marker. No STI.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 class="section-title">Lazy by default</h3>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">A component costs nothing until a
         value differs from its default — no row, no query. A new person with no
         bio has no row in <code>texts</code>, yet <code>user.bio_text</code> still
         works.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 class="section-title">Query by composition</h3>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">The posts list is
         <code>Post.with_component(State, prefix: :publish, status: "published")</code>
         — it filters by what an entity is made of, and scopes to the entity type
         so a shared component can't leak across types.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 class="section-title">One component, two slots</h3>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">A <%= link_to "group", groups_path %> has a
         name, a description <em>and</em> house rules — the same <code>Text</code>
         component declared three times under three slots. Same table, one row
         per slot, read as <code>group.rules_text</code>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3 class="section-title">Relationships & join entities</h3>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0"><code>relates_to :author, User</code>
         links a post to its author with no boilerplate. A
         <%= link_to "group", groups_path %> membership is a join <em>entity</em>
         carrying two relationships and a role.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3 class="section-title">Validation merges onto the entity</h3>
    <p class="prose" style="margin-bottom:0">The <code>Email</code> component
       validates its address; the error surfaces as
       <code>user.errors[:"email.address"]</code> and reads naturally in the form.
       Try <%= link_to "adding a person", new_user_path %> with a bad email.</p>
  </div>

  <p style="text-align:center;color:var(--faint);margin-top:1rem">
    Source & design docs on <a href="https://github.com/kranzky/ecs_rails">GitHub</a>,
    the gem on <a href="https://rubygems.org/gems/ecs_on_rails">RubyGems</a>.
  </p>
</div>
